---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/environmental-science/advanced/environmental-economics-and-policy">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    The Carbon Cycle and Climate Change and Ecosystems and Energy Flow taught the physics
    and the ecology, and the intermediate lessons — Air Quality &amp; Aerosols, Water
    Resources &amp; Pollution, Waste &amp; the Circular Economy, Energy Systems &amp; the
    Grid — catalogued the damages. This lesson asks what the science alone cannot answer:
    what should be done, at what cost, and who decides? It applies the externality logic of
    Market Failure &amp; Externalities to the environment and assembles the policy toolkit,
    from carbon prices to treaty design.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain externalities and the prescription to internalise them, through prices or through caps;</li>
    <li>run the present-value arithmetic behind the discount-rate dispute, and say which conclusions survive it;</li>
    <li>compare the instrument portfolio — pricing, standards, subsidies, information — and where each works;</li>
    <li>apply the three tests that decide whether an offset is real;</li>
    <li>trace the Kyoto-to-Paris evolution as a sequence of answers to the free-rider problem.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Environmental economics is <strong>the discipline of pricing what markets ignore</strong> —
    and environmental policy is its instruments in the political world. The science says "what";
    this field says "how much, who pays, and by which mechanism". The syllabus's
    atmosphere-and-human-activities unit names the case studies (4.2 climate, 4.3 acid rain,
    4.4 ozone) — and those three, it turns out, form a natural experiment in policy design:
    ozone is the solved triumph, acid rain the solved-to-order one, climate the unfinished
    endgame, and the differences between them are almost entirely economic and institutional
    rather than scientific. This page builds the instrument kit, runs the worked arithmetic,
    and extracts the design lessons the three cases teach.
  </p>
  <p>
    The core diagnosis: <strong>externalities</strong> — costs (or benefits) imposed on
    parties who did not choose them. A coal plant's electricity carries a price that
    omits the asthma, acid rain and warming its emissions cause, so the market supplies
    more coal than society would choose: the market fails not by malfunctioning but by
    functioning perfectly on incomplete prices. The economic prescription follows
    directly — <strong>internalise the externality</strong>: make the price include the
    damage, and let the market's own machinery do the choosing. Pigouvian taxes (after
    the economist Pigou) do it through price; cap-and-trade does it through quantity.
    The intellectual ancestor of the alternative route is Coase: with clear property
    rights and low negotiation costs, affected parties can bargain to the efficient
    outcome themselves — the theory behind tradable fishing quotas and village-level
    commons governance, and the reason Elinor Ostrom's work on communities managing
    their own resources (Nobel 2009) matters to policy design: institutions, not just
    instruments.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="pricing externalities, the Stern framing, the instruments, the international problem" widget="equationStep" config={envEconCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four steps move from diagnosis to hardest case.
    Step 1 is the lesson's engine — the same externality priced two ways, with the choice
    between tax and cap settled by political weather rather than arithmetic. Step 4 names
    the structural problem no single country can solve alone, which the treaty section
    returns to at the end.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the market fails not by malfunctioning but by pricing an
    incomplete world, and the prescription is to complete the price. The next section shows
    that even the arithmetic of "what is future damage worth today" hides an ethical choice.
  </p>

  <h2>The discount-rate war, with numbers</h2>
  <p>
    Every long-horizon policy question funnels into one parameter: how much should a
    cost in 2125 count today? The worked example makes the ethics visible:
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="call the magnitude — a century of discounting" widget="estimate" config={eepDiscountEstimate} />

  <Widget no="2" caption="present-value arithmetic — the parameter that quietly decides whether the future gets defended" widget="equationStep" config={discCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> change only the discount rate and the present value of
    the same century-ahead damage swings by a factor of several — nothing else in the
    calculation moves. That sensitivity is why the Stern–Nordhaus argument is a
    philosophical disagreement wearing a parameter's clothing, and why the social cost of
    carbon inherits the entire dispute.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Stern–Nordhaus dispute is exactly this. Stern's review chose a near-zero
    <em>pure</em> time preference (ethical argument: the year of your birth is no
    reason to count for less) plus modest risk adjustment → aggressive immediate
    action, mitigation costs ~1% of GDP against damages of 5–20%. Nordhaus's DICE
    model used market-observed rates → an "optimal" path of gradual ramping, and an
    SCC of tens rather than hundreds of dollars. Neither is arithmetic error; they
    are different answers to a philosophical question (how much do future people
    count?) wearing a parameter's clothing. The professional discipline is to know
    which conclusions <em>survive</em> the choice: aggressive near-term action is
    robust to the discount rate once uncertainty, tipping risks and the option value
    of not locking in infrastructure are included — gradualism is not.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="fill the blanks — two rates, two centuries" widget="cloze" config={eepSternCloze} />

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> one parameter quietly decides how urgent the future is, and the
    professional discipline is knowing which conclusions survive changing it. With the case
    for action sized, the question becomes practical: which instruments actually deliver it?
  </p>

  <h2>The instrument portfolio, and where each works</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Carbon pricing</strong> now covers roughly a quarter of global emissions
    (World Bank dashboard; ~75 instruments live). The two families trade off as the
    widget says: a <strong>tax</strong> fixes price, quantity floats; a
    <strong>cap</strong> fixes quantity, price floats. The flagship test was American
    acid rain: the 1990 Clean Air Act's Title IV capped SO₂ and let utilities trade
    allowances — emissions halved through the 1990s at a fraction of the predicted
    cost, the proof-of-concept that made the EU ETS possible. The EU ETS then taught
    the design lessons: over-allocation collapsed the price to under €5 (2017 — no
    scarcity, no signal), and the fix was institutional (a market stability reserve
    tightening supply); prices then climbed past €80–100 by 2022–23, finally in the
    range economists consider meaningful. Design details are where politics hides:
    free allocation versus auctioning (windfall profits), price collars (hybrids that
    bound the tax-cap dilemma), and border adjustments — the EU's <strong>CBAM</strong>
    (2023 onward), which taxes embedded carbon in imports both to protect domestic
    industry and to press free-riding trade partners, the first instrument aimed at
    the international problem directly.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="rebuild the cap-and-trade learning curve" widget="order" config={eepETSOrder} />
  <p>
    <strong>Regulation and standards</strong> (vehicle fuel-economy rules, appliance
    standards, building codes) often beat pricing politically and sometimes even
    economically: they exploit the fact that consumers under-invest in efficiency
    even when it pays (the "energy-efficiency gap" — information costs, landlord–
    tenant split incentives), and they remove the worst products from the market
    outright (the ozone protocol's approach). <strong>Subsidies and innovation
    policy</strong> work through the learning curve: solar module costs fell ~20%
    for every doubling of cumulative production (Wright's law) — a ~99.9% decline
    since 1976 — and deployment subsidies in Germany and China, by driving the
    doublings, bought the world its cheap solar; the same arithmetic now runs
    batteries and is attempting it for hydrogen and DAC. <strong>Information
    tools</strong> (labels, disclosure, task-force reporting) are cheap and
    surprisingly potent when capital markets price the signal. Real legislation is a
    portfolio: pricing for the broad signal, standards where behaviour is sticky,
    R&amp;D where the learning curve needs a push.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="which curve is Wright's law?" widget="trace" config={eepWrightTrace} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> cap-and-trade is a "licence to pollute" that lets the rich buy their way out. The cap fixes the total: trading only reallocates the reductions to whoever can make them cheapest, and the cap tightens over time — the acid-rain programme halved SO₂ emissions while utilities traded freely. Trading changes who reduces and at what cost; it cannot change the quantity the cap sets.</p>

  <h2>Offsets: the integrity problem</h2>
  <p>
    Offsetting — paying for reductions elsewhere — is economically elegant and
    empirically treacherous. Three tests decide whether a credit is real:
    <strong>additionality</strong> (would the forest have stood anyway? if the
    protection was going to happen regardless, the credit reduced nothing);
    <strong>permanence</strong> (a tree burns; a tonne avoided this year does not
    bank against future emissions); and <strong>leakage</strong> (protect one forest,
    the logging moves next door). The 2023 investigations into forest-offset
    programmes (Verra's rainforest credits, among others) found large fractions of
    the market failing the additionality test — baseline games, not physics fraud.
    The reform direction is structural: baselines set conservatively at jurisdiction
    scale (not project scale), reversal-risk buffers, and a hard separation between
    offsets used for <em>compensation claims</em> and the actual residual emissions a
    net-zero pathway still requires. The Paris Agreement's Article 6 exists to build
    exactly this plumbing internationally; its slowness is the subject's honest
    progress bar.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="pair each integrity test with its way of failing" widget="match" config={eepOffsetMatch} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> carbon pricing is unpopular, so it fails. The
    distributional design, not the price, decides survival: British Columbia's
    revenue-neutral carbon tax has run since 2008 with public support, while
    France's 2018 fuel-tax increase (no visible recycling, rural cost of living)
    detonated the gilets jaunes. The economics is silent on this — pricing is
    regressive unless revenues are returned ("carbon dividends" — returning revenue
    per capita makes the bottom deciles net winners in most analyses) — and the
    politics is decisive. The instruments that work are the ones that pay their
    own way publicly.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="you are pricing carbon — where does the money go?" widget="branch" config={eepRevenueBranch} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> instruments survive politically when their distributional
    design pays their own way publicly, and offsets count only after the integrity tests.
    But every instrument so far stops at the border — the next section takes on the
    free-rider problem itself.
  </p>

  <h2>The international endgame: from Kyoto to Paris</h2>
  <p>
    Climate is the hardest collective-action problem the international system has
    faced: every nation benefits from others' cuts whether or not it cuts itself
    (the free-rider structure), and the damages are global and delayed. The treaty
    history is a sequence of designs tested against that structure. <strong>Kyoto</strong>
    (1997) tried top-down binding targets for developed nations only — the US never
    ratified it, Canada quit, and global emissions rose; the design ignored the
    free-rider logic. <strong>Paris</strong> (2015) inverted it: no binding targets,
    but universal participation, nationally determined contributions, a five-yearly
    <strong>global stocktake</strong> and ratchet ("pledge-and-review") — betting that
    transparency, peer pressure and falling clean-technology costs would tighten
    ambition. The first stocktake (2023) returned the honest verdict: current pledges
    land near ~2.5–2.9 °C, well short of 1.5–2 °C, requiring roughly a doubling of
    ambition this decade. The complementary <strong>climate finance</strong> pillar
    ($100 billion/year promised to developing nations — delivered late (first met in
    2022 per OECD), now renegotiated upward; loss-and-damage fund agreed 2022) is
    where the equity question — polluters of the past versus emitters of the future —
    gets institutional form. The two solved cases bracket what success requires:
    the <strong>Montreal Protocol</strong> worked because few producers made few
    chemicals with ready substitutes (and a multilateral fund paid the developing
    world's transition — the finance principle in miniature); acid rain worked
    because damages were visible, costs local and substitutes cheap. Climate fails
    all three conditions — which is why its policy story is still being written.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="scrub the treaty record" widget="timeline" config={eepTreatyTimeline} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the Paris Agreement legally obliges countries to hit their targets. The binding parts are procedural — submit pledges, report transparently, resubmit; the nationally determined contributions themselves carry no enforcement mechanism. The design bets on transparency, peer pressure and ratcheting rather than compulsion, which is why the first global stocktake's shortfall verdict mattered more than any sanction could.</p>

  <h2>Political economy: durability and the club solution</h2>
  <p>
    The field's hardest problems are institutional, and two active ideas show the
    frontier. <strong>Just transition</strong> treats distribution as a design input,
    not a cleanup: coal regions that powered a century of growth carry concentrated
    losses from its decline, and policy that ignores them (mine closures without
    replacement wages, retraining, or place-based investment) manufactures the
    opposition that kills climate legislation — Germany's coal-commission model
    (negotiated exit dates with funded transition packages) is the template being
    copied. <strong>Climate clubs</strong> (Nordhaus's proposal) attack the free-rider
    structure directly: a coalition of ambitious countries prices carbon internally,
    applies border adjustments to outsiders, and shares technology benefits among
    members — turning the game from prisoner's dilemma (defect pays) into a club
    good (membership pays), the first proposed structure in which joining is a
    dominant strategy rather than a sacrifice. The G7 formally endorsed studying
    the design in 2022; whether it can hold against trade law and development
    politics is one of the decade's live experiments. The general lesson from both:
    the binding constraint on climate policy is rarely the optimal instrument — it
    is the construction of coalitions and institutions that keep instruments in
    place long enough for capital stock (power plants, buildings, vehicles) to
    turn over, the decade-scale clock the Kaya arithmetic runs on.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="two game boards — drag from dilemma to club" widget="compare" config={eepClubCompare} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Explain the difference between a carbon tax and cap-and-trade in terms of what is fixed and what floats — and what each one guarantees.</li>
    <li>Damage of USD 100 occurs in 2125. Using present value = future amount ÷ (1 + r) to the power of years, compare its present value at r = 3 per cent and at r = 1 per cent. Which policy posture does each number support?</li>
    <li>A forest-protection credit is sold as offsetting a tonne of emissions. Explain why it can fail to reduce emissions even if the forest really is protected, and name the test that catches this.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) A tax fixes the price and lets the quantity float — it
    guarantees the marginal cost of polluting but not the total emissions; a cap fixes the
    quantity and lets the price float — it guarantees the environmental outcome but not the
    cost. Trading, price collars and stability reserves are engineering built on that one
    choice. (2) At 3 per cent the present value is roughly 100 ÷ 1.03¹⁰⁰ ≈ 5; at 1 per cent
    it is roughly 37 — the same future damage is worth about seven times more, so the low
    rate makes aggressive immediate abatement look urgent while the high rate makes it look
    inefficient. The rate is an ethical position about how much future people count, not a
    measured constant. (3) Through <em>additionality</em>: if the protection was going to
    happen anyway — the forest was never under threat — the credit reduced nothing, however
    real the trees are. Permanence (the forest can still burn) and leakage (logging moves
    next door) are the other two tests.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Externalities are costs imposed on parties who never chose them; the fix is to internalise them by price or by quantity.</li>
    <li>The discount rate is an ethical parameter: present values of century-scale damage swing severalfold with it.</li>
    <li>Aggressive near-term action survives the discount-rate choice once uncertainty and tipping risks are counted; gradualism does not.</li>
    <li>Pricing, standards, subsidies and information work as a portfolio — each where behaviour is most responsive.</li>
    <li>Real offsets must pass additionality, permanence and leakage tests; many forest credits have failed the first.</li>
    <li>Distributional design decides political survival: recycled revenue wins support, invisible fuel taxes detonate.</li>
    <li>Paris replaced Kyoto's binding targets with pledge-and-review because free-riding defeated compulsion.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Climate Modelling &amp; Scenarios shows where the damage projections and carbon budgets
    feeding this cost–benefit machinery come from, and Sustainability Metrics &amp; LCA
    audits the removals and material claims that this lesson's offsets and instruments
    rely on.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Integrated assessment models and the Kaya decomposition.</strong> IAMs
    (DICE, FUND, PAGE) couple a carbon-cycle and climate module to an economic growth
    model, optimising (or simulating) the cost-benefit balance over centuries — the
    machinery behind SCC numbers and scenario cost estimates, and the subject of
    pointed critiques (fat-tailed catastrophic risk is awkward in optimisation;
    damage functions are empirically thin at high warming). The analytical backbone
    of every scenario is the <strong>Kaya identity</strong>:
    CO₂ = population × (GDP per capita) × (energy per GDP) × (CO₂ per energy) —
    four levers, each decomposable and projectable, and the standard way to see that
    decarbonisation is a race between growth and the two intensity terms. It turns
    the vague "we must change everything" into four measurable rates, one of which
    (carbon intensity of energy) has fallen globally ~1.5%/yr for decades — the
    number that must roughly triple for the Paris trajectories.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Valuing what has no price.</strong> When damages hit non-market goods,
    economics builds the prices: <strong>hedonic pricing</strong> (wage and property
    differentials for clean air), <strong>travel cost</strong> (recreation sites),
    <strong>contingent valuation</strong> (structured willingness-to-pay surveys —
    controversial, since stated preferences inflate), and <strong>damage-cost
    approaches</strong> (health impacts valued via statistical life estimates —
    themselves contested but unavoidable). The co-benefit finding — that air-quality
    gains from decarbonisation (the ~7 million annual deaths from fossil air
    pollution, per the air-quality chapter) pay back a large share of mitigation
    cost locally, immediately, in every region — is robust across valuation methods
    and is frequently the actual reason national policy moves, whatever the
    treaty language says.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> The field's live problems: pricing the
    <em>risk</em> of tipping points (standard CBA with a bell-curve damage function
    undervalues low-probability civilisation-scale outcomes — Weitzman's "dismal
    theorem" says fat tails can swamp any discount rate, an unresolved formal
    problem); the loss-and-damage architecture's financing; whether carbon removal
    can be scaled without eating the land/energy budget (the negative-emissions
    arithmetic the sustainability chapter audits); growth versus post-growth
    framings (can absolute decoupling run fast enough — the empirical debate the
    metrics chapter quantifies); and the political economy of durability — how to
    build climate policy that survives election cycles, the design question every
    solved case (ozone, acid rain) answered and every unsolved one is still
    arguing about.
  </p>

